nsidered a challenger, ranking second in the world before the Olympics."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Serbia opened the event with an Olympic best time of 1:20.99 in winning heat one, only to see that bettered by the German four in heat two, who posted 1:20.51. The unheralded Australian crew failed to qualify for the semi-finals in round one, and went to the quarter-finals, where they won the race in 1:19.39. In heat one of the semis, Australia again posted the fastest time, winning in 1:19.22."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The battle for the gold medal in the final came down to the favored Germans against the upstart Australians, with Germany posting a narrow win by 0.04 seconds. Behind them, Spain took the bronze medal. Although hardly considered a power in flatwater canoeing, this marked the 12th consecutive Olympics at which the Australian men had won a medal, a streak going back to 1980."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The German gold medalist had three paddlers who had been on the gold medal winning boat in 2020 – " [:a {:href "/athletes/134141"} "Max Rendschmidt"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/143395"} "Max Lemke"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/134143"} "Tom Liebscher-Lucz"] ". The fourth paddler was " [:a {:href "/athletes/143394"} "Jacob Schopf"] ", who replaced " [:a {:href "/athletes/90189"} "Ronny Rauhe"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "QF"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "Germany"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "1:20.51 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "1:20.57 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "1:19.80 (1 h1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "12"} [:a {:href "/athletes/134141"} "Max Ren